"""60-second summaries and typical page counts for book pages (keyed by cover slug).
Summaries state the book's core argument in 3 sentences or fewer, true to the book. Page counts: Open Library median."""
SUMMARIES = {
 "atomic-habits": ("Small habits compound into big results. James Clear argues you change behavior by fixing your systems, not your goals, and by building an identity (\"I am a reader\") that each habit proves. His 4 laws: make a habit obvious, attractive, easy and satisfying, and flip them to break a bad one.", 322),
 "deep-work": ("Cal Newport argues that focused, distraction-free work is becoming more valuable and more rare at the same time. Part 1 makes that case; Part 2 gives 4 rules: work deeply, embrace boredom, quit social media or use it on purpose, and drain the shallows. The goal is to make deep work a scheduled habit, not a mood.", 303),
 "indistractable": ("Nir Eyal says distraction isn't really about your phone: it's a way to escape uncomfortable feelings. His 4-part model: master your internal triggers, make time for what you value, hack back external triggers, and use pacts to make distraction harder.", 336),
 "digital-minimalism": ("Cal Newport argues for using a few tools on purpose instead of every app by default. The core is a 30-day digital declutter, followed by rebuilding your tech use around what you value. He fills the freed time with solitude, real conversation and demanding leisure.", 295),
 "meditations": ("The private notebook of Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius, never meant to be published. Again and again he reminds himself to focus on what he controls, to judge events less, to treat people kindly, and to remember that life is short.", 203),
 "the-obstacle-is-the-way": ("Ryan Holiday turns Stoic philosophy into a 3-step method for problems: perception (see it clearly, without panic), action (move on the part you can change, again and again), and will (accept what you can't change). The title is the idea: the obstacle becomes the path.", 224),
 "four-thousand-weeks": ("An average human life is about 4,000 weeks. Oliver Burkeman argues that no productivity trick can solve the problem of finite time, but accepting your limits can. Choose what to neglect, stop waiting for a future where you're on top of everything, and give your attention to what matters now.", 280),
 "the-psychology-of-money": ("Morgan Housel argues that doing well with money depends more on behavior than on intelligence. 20 short chapters show why: luck and risk shape outcomes, compounding needs time, wealth is the money you don't spend, and room for error beats a perfect plan.", 272),
 "i-will-teach-you-to-be-rich": ("Ramit Sethi lays out a 6-week program for running your money on autopilot. Set up good accounts, automate saving and investing on payday, invest in low-cost funds, then spend guilt-free on what you love and cut hard on what you don't.", 272),
 "your-money-or-your-life": ("Vicki Robin and Joe Dominguez treat money as life energy: the hours you trade for it. Their 9 steps have you track every penny, price spending in hours of your life, ask whether each purchase brought fulfillment, and build savings until investment income covers your costs.", 376),
 "the-power-of-habit": ("Charles Duhigg explains habits as a loop: a cue, a routine and a reward. You rarely kill a habit, so keep the cue and the reward and swap the routine. Some keystone habits, like exercise or a family dinner, pull other good habits along with them. The appendix turns this into 4 steps you can run tonight.", 371),
 "tiny-habits": ("BJ Fogg says behavior happens when motivation, ability and a prompt meet at the same moment. So make the habit tiny, anchor it right after something you already do, and celebrate the instant you do it. The good feeling is what wires it in, not repetition alone.", 320),
 "the-little-book-of-common-sense-investing": ("John C. Bogle, who founded Vanguard, argues that investors as a group can only earn the stock market's return, minus what they pay in fees, trading and taxes. So the winning move is to own the whole market through a low-cost index fund and hold it for decades. The rest of the book backs that up with data on costs, fund chasing and how to split stocks and bonds.", 270),
 "essentialism": ("Greg McKeown argues that trying to do everything means doing nothing well. The Essentialist does less but better: pick the few things that matter, cut the rest with a graceful no, and make the important work easy with buffers and routines. The book runs in 4 parts: essence, explore, eliminate and execute.", 272),
}
